export default {"id":"sounddesign-level-3","number":3,"title":"Filters","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Define the fundamental principle of subtractive synthesis: frequency-selective attenuation of harmonically rich source signals. - Define and control the Filter Cutoff Frequency (f_c) as the primary pivot point of spectral balance.","modules":[{"id":"sounddesign-m-3-1-1","code":"3.1","title":"The Subtractive Idea","blurb":"- Define the fundamental principle of subtractive synthesis: frequency-selective attenuation of harmonically rich source signals. - Define and control the Filter Cutoff Frequency (f_c) as the primary pivot point of spectral balance.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-7","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Define the fundamental principle of <strong>subtractive synthesis</strong>: frequency-selective attenuation of harmonically rich source signals.","Define and control the <strong>Filter Cutoff Frequency (f_c)</strong> as the primary pivot point of spectral balance.","Calculate and differentiate <strong>Filter Slopes</strong> across 1-pole (6 dB/oct), 2-pole (12 dB/oct), 3-pole (18 dB/oct), and 4-pole (24 dB/oct) configurations.","Control <strong>Resonance (Q)</strong> to accentuate frequencies adjacent to the cutoff point and demonstrate <strong>self-oscillation</strong> as a pure sine wave tone generator.","Differentiate between a synthesis filter (dynamic musical sculpting) and a parametric equalizer (static corrective frequency balancing)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-1-1","title":"3.1.1 — Subtractive Synthesis in One Sentence","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.1 — Subtractive Synthesis in One Sentence","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Subtractive synthesis</strong> is the acoustic process of generating a complex, harmonically dense waveform (such as a sawtooth or square wave) and deliberately attenuating or removing unwanted frequency regions using frequency-selective filters."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ HARMONICALLY DENSE RAW SOURCE ] ===&gt; [ SPECTRAL FILTER ] ===&gt; [ SHAPED TIMBRAL OUTPUT ]\n     (Sawtooth: All Harmonics)            (Removes Highs)             (Warm, Muted Tone)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"The synthesizer does not \"build\" a warm sound by adding low frequencies; it starts with a sound that has <em>everything</em> and carves away the high-frequency energy until only the desired body remains."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.1 (The Subtractive Principle)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2 &amp; §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-1-2","title":"3.1.2 — What a Filter Actually Does","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.2 — What a Filter Actually Does","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A filter is a frequency-dependent attenuator. Unlike an overall volume control (which lowers the amplitude of all frequencies equally across the board), a filter applies varying degrees of volume reduction depending entirely on the frequency of the incoming audio."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Overall Volume Fader:\nAmplitude (dB)\n  0 dB | --------------------------- (Before Fader)\n -6 dB | - - - - - - - - - - - - - - (After Fader: All Frequencies Dropped by 6 dB)\n       +----------------------------&gt; Frequency\n\nLow-Pass Filter:\nAmplitude (dB)\n  0 dB | =============\\              (Lows Pass Through Untouched)\n       |               \\\n       |                \\            (Highs Attenuated Increasingly)\n-48 dB +-----------------\\----------&gt; Frequency\n                      Cutoff (fc)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Passband:</strong> The frequency region that travels through the filter with zero or negligible attenuation (0 dB).","<strong>Stopband:</strong> The frequency region that is actively attenuated and suppressed by the filter.","<strong>Transition Band:</strong> The slope region between the passband and stopband where attenuation progressively increases."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Synthesis – 3 – Filter</em>, Chapter 1 (Filter Mechanics)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-1-3","title":"3.1.3 — Cutoff Frequency as the Hinge","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.3 — Cutoff Frequency as the Hinge","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Cutoff Frequency (f_c)</strong> is the exact point in the frequency spectrum where the filter begins its active attenuation. By international engineering convention, the cutoff frequency is precisely defined as the <strong>-3 dB point</strong>: the frequency at which the signal power has dropped by half (3 dB down from the passband level)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (dB)\n  0 dB | ===================\\\n       |                     \\\n -3 dB | . . . . . . . . . . .\\  &lt;--- [ CUTOFF FREQUENCY (fc) ]\n       |                       \\\n       |                        \\\n       +-------------------------+--&gt; Frequency\n                                 fc</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Musical Impact of Cutoff Movement","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Sweeping Cutoff Up (100 Hz → 15 kHz):</strong> Reveals higher and higher overtones, causing the sound to transform from dark, muffled, and distant into bright, present, and aggressive.","<strong>Sweeping Cutoff Down (15 kHz → 100 Hz):</strong> Progressively eliminates upper harmonics, muting the sound until only the deep fundamental sine-like tone remains."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.2 (The Cutoff Hinge)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-1-4","title":"3.1.4 — Filter Slope / Poles: 6, 12, 18, and 24 dB per Octave","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.4 — Filter Slope / Poles: 6, 12, 18, and 24 dB per Octave","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>slope</strong> (or steepness) of a filter dictates how aggressively frequencies are attenuated once they cross the cutoff point into the stopband. Filter slope is measured in <strong>decibels of attenuation per octave (dB/oct)</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"In analog circuit design, each individual resistor-capacitor (RC) filter circuit contributes a slope of 6 dB/octave and is termed a <strong>Pole</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+----------+------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------+\n| Filter   | Attenuation| Historical Circuit    | Timbral Character &amp; Use Case          |\n| Order    | Slope      | Heritage              |                                       |\n+----------+------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------+\n| **1-Pole**| $6\\text{ dB/oct}$ | Vintage passive filters| Extremely gentle, smooth roll-off.    |\n|          |            |                       | Natural acoustic instrument warmth.   |\n+----------+------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------+\n| **2-Pole**| $12\\text{ dB/oct}$| Oberheim SEM, Polysynths| Musical, open, bright. Lets mid-high  |\n|          |            |                       | harmonics bleed through. Great for pads.|\n+----------+------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------+\n| **3-Pole**| $18\\text{ dB/oct}$| Roland TB-303 (Acid)  | Distinct, rubbery, aggressive curve.   |\n|          |            |                       | Classic squelchy acid basslines.      |\n+----------+------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------+\n| **4-Pole**| $24\\text{ dB/oct}$| Moog Ladder, Classic Sub| Steep, clinical, dramatic cut. Closes |\n|          |            |                       | sound down tightly. King of fat bass. |\n+----------+------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Visual Slope Comparison (Low-Pass Filter at fc = 1 kHz):\n  0 dB | ===================\\\n       |                     \\\n-12 dB |                      \\-- [1-Pole: -6 dB/oct]\n-24 dB |                       \\--- [2-Pole: -12 dB/oct]\n-36 dB |                        \\---- [3-Pole: -18 dB/oct]\n-48 dB |                         \\----- [4-Pole: -24 dB/oct]\n       +---------------------+----+----+----+----&gt; Frequency\n                           1 kHz 2 kHz 4 kHz 8 kHz</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["At 1 octave above cutoff (2 kHz): A 4-pole filter attenuates the signal by -24 dB; a 2-pole filter attenuates it by only -12 dB."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Synthesis – 3 – Filter</em>, Chapter 2 (Filter Slopes &amp; Poles)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-1-5","title":"3.1.5 — Resonance / Q: The Peak at the Hinge","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.5 — Resonance / Q: The Peak at the Hinge","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Resonance</strong> (often labeled <strong>Q</strong> for <em>Quality Factor</em>) is an internal feedback mechanism in the filter circuit. A portion of the filtered audio output is fed back into the filter input, creating a narrow resonant boost right at the cutoff frequency (f_c)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (dB)\n+12 dB |                      /\\   &lt;--- [ RESONANCE PEAK (Q) ]\n       |                     /  \\\n  0 dB | ===================/    \\\n       |                          \\\n-24 dB |                           \\\n       +----------------------------+---&gt; Frequency\n                                    fc</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Behavior of Resonance:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Low Resonance (Q = 0.0 - 0.2):</strong> Clean, transparent frequency removal with zero coloration or ringing.","<strong>Medium Resonance (Q = 0.4 - 0.7):</strong> Creates an audible \"vocal\", \"nasal\", or \"wah\" quality as the cutoff moves across formants.","<strong>High Resonance (Q &gt; 0.85):</strong> Creates a sharp, piercing, whistling harmonic peak that emphasizes whatever specific note or overtone the cutoff frequency aligns with."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Important Gain Note:</strong> In many analog-modeled filters, increasing resonance reduces the volume of the passband low-end (known as <em>low-end drop</em> on Moog-style circuits). Modern digital synths (<code>Vital</code>, <code>Surge XT</code>) often feature resonance compensation switches to prevent low-end thinning."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.3 (Resonance &amp; Q Factor)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-1-6","title":"3.1.6 — Self-Oscillation: The Filter Becomes a Sine Wave Oscillator","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.6 — Self-Oscillation: The Filter Becomes a Sine Wave Oscillator","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the resonance (Q) of a 4-pole or 2-pole analog-modeled filter is pushed to its absolute maximum limit (100% / Q 1.0), the internal feedback loop achieves unity gain. "},{"type":"prose","html":"At this point, the filter <strong>self-oscillates</strong>: it emits a pure, whistling <strong>Sine Wave</strong> at the exact frequency of the cutoff point, <strong>even if the oscillators are completely turned off</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ NO OSCILLATOR INPUT (Muted) ] ===&gt; [ FILTER: Resonance = 100% ] ===&gt; [ PURE SINE WAVE OUT ]\n                                                 |\n                                       Frequency = Filter Cutoff (fc)</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-Oscillating Filter Patch (Synthetic Sine Generator)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In <code>Analog</code> or <code>Drift</code>, mute Oscillator 1 and Oscillator 2.","Set Filter to <strong>Low-Pass 24 dB</strong> and push <strong>Resonance to 100%</strong>.","Enable <strong>Keytracking (100% / 1.00)</strong> on the Filter so the filter cutoff tracks incoming MIDI keyboard pitches.","Play the keyboard. The filter acts as a pure, mathematically perfect, pitch-tracked sine wave oscillator."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.4 (Self-Oscillating Filters)","<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Analog Filter Section)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-1-7","title":"3.1.7 — Filter vs. EQ: Timbral Sculpting vs. Precision Balancing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.7 — Filter vs. EQ: Timbral Sculpting vs. Precision Balancing","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A sound designer must understand the distinct operational roles of a <strong>Synthesizer Filter</strong> versus a <strong>Parametric Equalizer (EQ)</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n|        SYNTHESIZER FILTER          |       PARAMETRIC EQUALIZER (EQ)    |\n|   (Dynamic Sound Design Tool)      |      (Static Mixing Balance Tool)  |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n| - Integrated inside the synth core | - Inserted as a post-synth effect  |\n| - Dynamic: Modulated continuously  | - Static: Set to correct frequency |\n|   by ADSR envelopes, LFOs, and MIDI|   imbalances across a track        |\n| - High resonance / non-linear drive| - Clean, minimum-phase or linear-  |\n|   circuits that add harmonic color |   phase curves designed for clarity|\n| - Steep global slopes (12/24 dB)   | - Precise surgical bell curves     |\n|   shaping the overall timbre       |   ($Q$-notches to cut mud/rumble)  |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"You use the <strong>Synth Filter</strong> to make a sound <em>be</em> a pluck or a dark bass. You use the <strong>EQ</strong> (<code>EQ Eight</code>) to carve out a -3 dB pocket at 300 Hz so the bass does not mask the snare drum."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.5 (Filter vs. EQ)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §13 &amp; §15"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-3-2-2","code":"3.2","title":"The Big Four Filter Types","blurb":"- Operate and identify the four primary filter topologies: Low-Pass (LPF), High-Pass (HPF), Band-Pass (BPF), and Notch / Band-Reject (BRF). - Analyze specialty filter circuits including Comb Filters (delay-based phase peaks) and Formant Fil","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-8","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Operate and identify the four primary filter topologies: <strong>Low-Pass (LPF)</strong>, <strong>High-Pass (HPF)</strong>, <strong>Band-Pass (BPF)</strong>, and <strong>Notch / Band-Reject (BRF)</strong>.","Analyze specialty filter circuits including <strong>Comb Filters</strong> (delay-based phase peaks) and <strong>Formant Filters</strong> (vocal tract resonance modeling).","Leverage <strong>State-Variable Filters (SVF)</strong> to continuously morph between filter topologies in real-time (<code>Drift</code>, <code>Surge XT</code>).","Apply <strong>Filter Keytracking</strong> to maintain consistent harmonic brightness across multi-octave keyboard performances.","Balance pre-filter and post-filter <strong>Drive / Saturation</strong> circuits to inject aggressive harmonic color."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-1","title":"3.2.1 — Low-Pass (LPF): The Workhorse","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.1 — Low-Pass (LPF): The Workhorse","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Low-Pass Filter (LPF)</strong> is the fundamental building block of electronic music synthesis. It passes all frequencies below the cutoff point (f_c) without alteration while progressively attenuating all frequencies above the cutoff."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (dB)\n  0 dB | ===================\\\n       |      PASSBAND       \\       STOPBAND\n       |  (Lows / Mids Pass)  \\   (Highs Attenuated)\n-48 dB +-----------------------\\------------------&gt; Frequency\n                               fc</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Production Applications","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Synth Basses:</strong> Set f_c between 150 Hz - 400 Hz to eliminate harsh top-end buzz and focus low-end acoustic weight.","<strong>Plucks:</strong> Modulate f_c with a sharp decay envelope so the note strikes brightly and immediately darkens.","<strong>Warm Pads:</strong> Roll off highs above 3 kHz to seat atmospheric pads behind bright lead vocals."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.6 (LPF Topologies)","<em>PML Synthesis – 3 – Filter</em>, Chapter 3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-2","title":"3.2.2 — High-Pass (HPF): Thin Tops, Risers, Mud-Remover","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.2 — High-Pass (HPF): Thin Tops, Risers, Mud-Remover","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>High-Pass Filter (HPF)</strong> is the exact inverse of a low-pass filter. It passes all frequencies above the cutoff point (f_c) while attenuating all frequencies below it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (dB)\n  0 dB |                /===================\n       |    STOPBAND   /       PASSBAND\n       | (Lows Cut)   /   (Highs / Air Pass)\n-48 dB +-------------/---------------------&gt; Frequency\n                     fc</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Production Applications","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Mud Cleanup on Leads/Pads:</strong> Set f_c between 100 Hz - 250 Hz on leads, keys, and pads to eliminate sub-bass rumble that would otherwise clash with the dedicated bassline and kick drum.","<strong>Noise Sweeps / Risers:</strong> Sweep f_c upwards from 200 Hz to 10 kHz during track build-ups to thin out energy before the drop.","<strong>Hi-Hats &amp; Cymbals:</strong> Cut low and low-mid noise (&lt;3 kHz) to leave only ultra-crisp, metallic top sizzle."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.7 (HPF Topologies)","<em>PML Synthesis – 3 – Filter</em>, Chapter 4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-3","title":"3.2.3 — Band-Pass (BPF): Telephone, Vocal Formants, Mid Focus","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.3 — Band-Pass (BPF): Telephone, Vocal Formants, Mid Focus","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Band-Pass Filter (BPF)</strong> combines a high-pass and low-pass slope, attenuating frequencies both above <em>and</em> below a central resonant passband. Only a narrow \"band\" of frequencies centered at f_c passes through."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (dB)\n  0 dB |              /\\\n       |  STOPBAND   /  \\    STOPBAND\n       | (Lows Cut) /    \\  (Highs Cut)\n-48 dB +-----------/------\\----------------&gt; Frequency\n                          fc</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Production Applications","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Telephone / Transistor Radio Effect:</strong> Set f_c to ≈ 1.5 kHz with a moderate bandwidth to isolate narrow speech frequencies.","<strong>Vocal-like Growls &amp; Leads:</strong> Sweeping a resonant BPF across a distorted saw wave creates vocal \"ow-ow\" and \"wah\" formant movements.","<strong>Dub Chords &amp; Tech Stabs:</strong> Placing a BPF across a minor chord creates a dense, focused mid-range stab that punches through busy percussion without muddying the sub."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.8 (BPF Topologies)","<em>PML Synthesis – 3 – Filter</em>, Chapter 5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-4","title":"3.2.4 — Notch (Band-Reject): Phaser-Like Scoops, Surgical Removal","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.4 — Notch (Band-Reject): Phaser-Like Scoops, Surgical Removal","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Notch Filter</strong> (also called a <strong>Band-Reject</strong> or <strong>Band-Stop Filter</strong>) is the inverse of a band-pass filter. It passes all low and high frequencies across the entire spectrum, carving out only a very narrow, localized band of frequencies centered at f_c."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (dB)\n  0 dB | =======\\            /=======\n       |         \\  NOTCH   /\n       |          \\ (Cut)  /\n-48 dB +-----------\\______/---------&gt; Frequency\n                          fc</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Production Applications","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Phaser Emulation:</strong> Modulating the cutoff frequency of a notch filter with an LFO produces sweeping phase cancellations that mimic vintage analog phasers.","<strong>Resonance Carving:</strong> Notch filters can hollow out harsh nasal frequencies (≈ 3 kHz) without altering the low-end weight or high-frequency shimmer."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.9 (Notch Topologies)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-5","title":"3.2.5 — Comb Filters: Delay-Based Resonant Peaks","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.5 — Comb Filters: Delay-Based Resonant Peaks","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Comb Filter</strong> operates by mixing an incoming signal with a micro-delayed copy of itself (typically 0.1 ms - 10 ms). This produces a series of regularly spaced constructive (peaks) and destructive (notches) phase interference points that resemble the teeth of a physical comb."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (dB)\n  0 dB |   /\\    /\\    /\\    /\\    /\\   (Series of regularly spaced resonant peaks)\n       |  /  \\  /  \\  /  \\  /  \\  /  \\\n-36 dB +-+----+----+----+----+----+----+----&gt; Frequency</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["When resonance/feedback is applied to a comb filter, the peaks turn into ringing pitched tones (Karplus-Strong physical modeling).","Essential in modern aggressive bass design (<code>Vital</code>, <code>Surge XT</code>) to impart metallic, flanged, and robotic resonance to growls."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 4 (Filter Section: Comb Models)","<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 4 (Filter Section: Comb &amp; Flange)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-6","title":"3.2.6 — Formant / Vowel Filters","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.6 — Formant / Vowel Filters","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Formant Filter</strong> models the physical acoustic filtering of the human vocal tract. The human mouth and throat shape noise into distinct vowels (A, E, I, O, U) by creating two or three simultaneous resonant bandpass peaks called <strong>formants</strong> (F_1, F_2, F_3)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Human Vocal Tract \"AH\" Vowel Profile:\nAmplitude (dB)\n  0 dB |         /\\              /\\\n       |        /  \\            /  \\\n-24 dB +-------+----+----------+----+-----&gt; Frequency\n              F1 (800 Hz)     F2 (1.2 kHz)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"By modulating a Formant filter between vowel coordinates via an LFO or envelope, synthesizers can make synthetic waveforms speak, grunt, and talk (vital for dubstep growls and expressive leads)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 4 (Formant Filter Models)","<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 4 (Vowel Filter Engine)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-7","title":"3.2.7 — State-Variable Filters (SVF): Continuous Morphing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.7 — State-Variable Filters (SVF): Continuous Morphing","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>State-Variable Filter (SVF)</strong> is an analog circuit design (implemented digitally in <code>Drift</code> and <code>Surge XT</code>) that calculates low-pass, high-pass, and band-pass outputs simultaneously."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ IN ] ===&gt; [ STATE-VARIABLE FILTER CORE ] ===&gt; Low-Pass (LP) Output\n                       |\n                       +======================&gt; Band-Pass (BP) Output\n                       |\n                       +======================&gt; High-Pass (HP) Output</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Instead of clicking a hard switch between filter types, an SVF features a continuous <strong>Morph</strong> knob: LPF (0%) Notch / BPF (50%) HPF (100%)"},{"type":"prose","html":"Modulating the filter <em>type</em> dynamically with an envelope or LFO allows a patch to seamlessly transition from a dark bass pluck into a thin high-frequency sizzle within a single held note."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Drift Filter Section)","<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 4 (SVF Architecture)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-8","title":"3.2.8 — Filter Keytracking: Scaling Brightness with Pitch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.8 — Filter Keytracking: Scaling Brightness with Pitch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When you play higher notes on a keyboard, the fundamental frequency moves upwards. If your filter cutoff remains statically locked at 500 Hz, high notes (e.g., C_5 ≈ 523 Hz) will have their fundamental frequencies severely attenuated, making the upper register sound quiet and muffled."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Filter Keytracking</strong> (or <em>Key Follow</em>) links the filter cutoff frequency to the incoming MIDI note pitch:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ MIDI NOTE NUMBER ] ===(Keytrack Amount: 0% to 100%)===&gt; [ FILTER CUTOFF (fc) ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Keytrack Setting | Behavior Across the Keyboard                               |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **$0\\%$ (Off)**  | Cutoff frequency remains static regardless of note played. |\n|                  | High notes get heavily filtered and choked.                |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **$100\\%$ (1:1)**| Cutoff tracks keyboard pitch in exact musical semitones.   |\n|                  | Timbral brightness and harmonic balance remain perfectly   |\n|                  | identical across all octaves.                              |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Standard Rule:</strong> Always set Keytracking to 100% on leads, polyphonic pads, and acoustic emulations. Leave Keytracking at 0% on sub-bass patches where you want high notes to be choked to preserve low-end focus."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.10 (Filter Keytracking)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-2-9","title":"3.2.9 — Filter Drive and Saturation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.9 — Filter Drive and Saturation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Many synthesizer filters include integrated <strong>Drive</strong> or <strong>Saturation</strong> circuits modeled after analog component over-driving:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Pre-Filter Drive:\n[ OSC ] ===&gt; [ DRIVE / SATURATION ] ===&gt; [ FILTER ] ===&gt; [ AMP ]\n              (Adds New Harmonics)        (Filters the Added Harmonics)\n\nPost-Filter Drive:\n[ OSC ] ===&gt; [ FILTER ] ===&gt; [ DRIVE / SATURATION ] ===&gt; [ AMP ]\n             (Smooths Tone)   (Distorts the Filtered Signal &amp; Peak)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pre-Filter Drive:</strong> Generates aggressive harmonic saturation <em>before</em> filtering. The filter then sculpts the newly created distortion overtones, resulting in a thick, controlled, analog warmth.","<strong>Post-Filter Drive:</strong> Distorts the resonance peak of the filter, turning sharp whistling resonance into screaming, aggressive growls (classic in acid and neurofunk basslines)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Analog / Drift Drive Modes)","<em>PML Synthesis – 3 – Filter</em>, Chapter 6"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-3-3-3","code":"3.3","title":"Frequency as a Designer's Map","blurb":"- Map and navigate the seven functional frequency bands across the audible acoustic spectrum (20 Hz - 20 kHz). - Identify, diagnose, and eliminate acoustic masking in the Low-Mid Mud Zone (200 Hz - 400 Hz).","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-9","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Map and navigate the seven functional frequency bands across the audible acoustic spectrum (20 Hz - 20 kHz).","Identify, diagnose, and eliminate acoustic masking in the <strong>Low-Mid Mud Zone</strong> (200 Hz - 400 Hz).","Apply the <strong>Fletcher-Munson Equal-Loudness Contours</strong> to control the psychoacoustic <strong>Harshness Zone</strong> (2 kHz - 5 kHz).","Deploy <code>EQ Eight</code> and <code>Channel EQ</code> as real-time sound design sculpting devices directly following instruments.","Complete the <strong>Gate B Self-Assessment Checkpoint</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-3-1","title":"3.3.1 — The Seven Frequency Bands","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.1 — The Seven Frequency Bands","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A professional sound designer must internalize the audible spectrum not as an abstract slider, but as seven distinct acoustic territories with dedicated roles and physical boundaries:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                  THE SEVEN FREQUENCY BANDS                                        |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| BAND NAME         | FREQUENCY RANGE             | ACOUSTIC CHARACTER &amp; MIX ROLE                   |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **1. Sub Bass**   | $20\\text{ Hz} - 60\\text{ Hz}$| Pure physical weight and pressure; felt rather  |\n|                   |                             | than heard. Keep mono and unmodulated.         |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **2. Low End / Bass**| $60\\text{ Hz} - 200\\text{ Hz}$| Punch, body of kick drum and bassline.          |\n|                   |                             | Defines foundational tonal pitch.               |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **3. Low-Mids**   | $200\\text{ Hz} - 500\\text{ Hz}$| Warmth, thickness, woodiness, body. Danger      |\n|                   |                             | zone for clutter, boxiness, and mud.            |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **4. Midrange**   | $500\\text{ Hz} - 2\\text{ kHz}$| Core musical presence; vocal intelligibility,   |\n|                   |                             | synth body, snare fundamental crack.            |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **5. High-Mids**  | $2\\text{ kHz} - 5\\text{ kHz}$| Attack transient, crunch, bite, edge, vocal     |\n|                   |                             | presence. Maximum human ear sensitivity.        |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **6. Highs / Sizzle**| $5\\text{ kHz} - 10\\text{ kHz}$| Metallic brightness, hi-hat shimmer, pluck     |\n|                   |                             | snap, acoustic string scrape.                   |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+\n| **7. Air / Top**  | $10\\text{ kHz} - 20\\text{ kHz}$| Openness, luxury gloss, breathing room, spatial|\n|                   |                             | dimension, subtle white noise sheen.            |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">20 Hz       60 Hz      200 Hz       500 Hz      2 kHz       5 kHz       10 kHz      20 kHz\n  |---Sub----|----Bass---|--Low-Mid---|---Midrange---|--High-Mid--|---Highs---|---Air---|</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Frequency Cheat Sheet for Audio Mixing</em>, Sections 1–7","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §7 &amp; §15"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-3-2","title":"3.3.2 — Where Instruments Actually Live","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.2 — Where Instruments Actually Live","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When designing a patch, you must know what acoustic territory the sound is expected to occupy before you begin turning knobs:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------+\n| Sound Role       | Primary Weight Band   | Harmonic Bite / Air   | High-Pass / Low-Pass Cut   |\n+------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------+\n| **Sub Bass**     | $30\\text{ Hz}-60\\text{ Hz}$| Clean Sine (None)     | LPF @ $100\\text{ Hz}$      |\n+------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------+\n| **Mid Bass**     | $90\\text{ Hz}-250\\text{ Hz}$| $800\\text{ Hz}-3\\text{ kHz}$| HPF @ $90\\text{ Hz}$ (Split)|\n+------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------+\n| **Synth Pluck**  | $300\\text{ Hz}-800\\text{ Hz}$| $2\\text{ kHz}-8\\text{ kHz}$ | HPF @ $150\\text{ Hz}$      |\n+------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------+\n| **Pads / Beds**  | $250\\text{ Hz}-1\\text{ kHz}$| $6\\text{ kHz}-12\\text{ kHz}$| HPF @ $200\\text{ Hz}$      |\n+------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------+\n| **Leads**        | $400\\text{ Hz}-1.5\\text{ kHz}$| $3\\text{ kHz}-10\\text{ kHz}$| HPF @ $180\\text{ Hz}$      |\n+------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------+\n| **Hi-Hats / Shakers**| —                  | $6\\text{ kHz}-16\\text{ kHz}$| HPF @ $3\\text{ kHz}-6\\text{ kHz}$|\n+------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Frequency Cheat Sheet</em>, Table 1 (Instrument Frequency Mapping)","<em>Dubstep-Production-Beginner-Live12-Launchkey.md</em>, §6.1–6.4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-3-3","title":"3.3.3 — Mud Danger Zone: The 200–400 Hz Collision","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.3 — Mud Danger Zone: The 200–400 Hz Collision","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The single most common flaw in electronic sound design and mixing is the accumulation of unmanaged energy between <strong>200 Hz and 400 Hz</strong> (the <em>Low-Mid Mud Zone</em>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude (dB)\n  0 dB |           /=========\\  &lt;--- [ MUD COLLISION: Bass Overtones + Pad Body\n       |          /  COLLISION\\        + Synth Pluck Fund + Snare Body All Competing ]\n       |         /             \\\n-24 dB +--------+---------------+--------------------+---&gt; Frequency\n              200 Hz          400 Hz</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Causes and Cures","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Cause:</strong> Every subtractive synth pad, chord stab, guitar sample, and mid-bass naturally generates its lower fundamental and second harmonics right in this 200-400 Hz window. When four synths play together, this band overloads, making the mix sound \"cloudy,\" \"cardboard,\" and indistinct.","<strong>The Sound Design Cure:</strong> When designing polyphonic pads or secondary leads, use an <code>EQ Eight</code> or the synth's internal high-pass filter to aggressively carve away 200-350 Hz content. Keep this region lean so the primary bass and snare body can breathe."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>PML Frequency Cheat Sheet</em>, Section 3 (Low-Mid Management)","<em>Drum-and-Bass-Production-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §7 &amp; §9"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-3-4","title":"3.3.4 — Harshness Zone: The 2–5 kHz Ear Sensitivity Peak","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.4 — Harshness Zone: The 2–5 kHz Ear Sensitivity Peak","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Due to the physical acoustic resonance of the human ear canal, human hearing is psychoacoustically hyper-sensitive to the frequency band between <strong>2.5 kHz and 4.5 kHz</strong> (the <em>Fletcher-Munson Equal-Loudness Curve</em>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Fletcher-Munson Sensitivity Curve:\nSensitivity\n  High |                  /\\   &lt;--- [ MAXIMUM HUMAN EAR SENSITIVITY: 2.5 kHz - 4.5 kHz ]\n       |                 /  \\       (Sounds here feel 6-10 dB louder than they actually are)\n   Low | \\              /    \\\n       +--+------------+------+---------------------&gt; Frequency\n        50 Hz        2.5 kHz 4.5 kHz              20 kHz</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Engineering Implications:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Boosting 3 kHz adds instant, aggressive presence, snap, and cutting power.","However, excessive resonance or harsh distortion peaks in the 2-5 kHz range cause immediate listener ear fatigue, shrillness, and pain at club listening levels.","<strong>Design Habit:</strong> Always use <code>Spectrum</code> to inspect sharp resonant spikes between 2.5 kHz-4.5 kHz. Tame them with a narrow notch or dip if they ring harshly."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.11 (Psychoacoustics &amp; Fletcher-Munson)","<em>PML Frequency Cheat Sheet</em>, Section 5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-3-5","title":"3.3.5 — EQ Eight as a Design-Time Carving Tool","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.5 — EQ Eight as a Design-Time Carving Tool","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Ableton Live 12 Standard, <code>EQ Eight</code> is not merely a mixing utility inserted during the final mixdown; it is a vital <strong>design-time sculpting device</strong> placed directly after your synthesizer."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ SYNTH (Drift / Vital) ] ===&gt; [ EQ EIGHT (Design Sculpting) ] ===&gt; [ SATURATOR / EFFECTS ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+--------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| EQ Eight Filter Node| Typical Filter Topology| Sound Design Function                  |\n+--------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **Band 1**         | High-Pass ($48\\text{ dB/oct}$)| Removes subsonic DC rumble ($&lt;30\\text{ Hz}$)|\n+--------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **Band 2**         | Low Bell ($Q = 1.0$)    | Dips low-mid mud ($-2.5\\text{ dB} @ 300\\text{ Hz}$)|\n+--------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **Band 3 &amp; 4**     | Parametric Bell         | Sharp notch to remove ringing resonance|\n+--------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| **Band 8**         | High Shelf ($10\\text{ kHz}$)| Adds smooth high-end \"air\" (+2 dB)     |\n+--------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Using <code>EQ Eight</code> at design time guarantees that the audio entering downstream distortion, chorus, and reverb effects is already clean, focused, and free of clashing low-end artifacts."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 28 (Audio Effect Reference: EQ Eight)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §13"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-3-6","title":"3.3.6 — Channel EQ as Fast Tone Test","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.6 — Channel EQ as Fast Tone Test","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"For instantaneous, two-second frequency shaping without opening complex parametric windows, Ableton Live 12 includes <strong><code>Channel EQ</code></strong> (modeled after classic British console channel strips)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            ABLETON CHANNEL EQ CONTROLS                        |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **HP 80 Hz Toggle**  | Instant $80\\text{ Hz}$ high-pass filter to clean sub.  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Low Shelf Knob**   | Boosts or cuts low-end body at $100\\text{ Hz}$ ($\\pm 15\\text{ dB}$).|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Mid Sweep Knob**   | Semi-parametric bell: Sweeps center frequency          |\n|                      | ($120\\text{ Hz} - 7.5\\text{ kHz}$) with $\\pm 15\\text{ dB}$ gain.|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **High Shelf Knob**  | Gentle, musical top-end air shelf at $8.5\\text{ kHz}$. |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"When sketching a patch quickly, drop in <code>Channel EQ</code>, click the <code>HP 80 Hz</code> button, and use the Mid sweep knob to instantly locate and remove boxiness."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 28 (Audio Effect Reference: Channel EQ)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-3-7","title":"3.3.7 — Ear Quiz Prep: Blind Filter Type and Slope Identification","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.7 — Ear Quiz Prep: Blind Filter Type and Slope Identification","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To pass <strong>Gate B</strong>, you must be able to identify filter types and slopes purely by listening to a white noise or sawtooth source:"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Decision Rules:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Low-Pass Filter (LPF):</strong> The sound becomes progressively darker and muffled as cutoff drops. High-frequency sizzle completely disappears.","<strong>High-Pass Filter (HPF):</strong> The sound becomes progressively thinner, tinny, and lightweight as cutoff rises. Low-end body and sub weight completely disappear.","<strong>Band-Pass Filter (BPF):</strong> The sound feels focused, narrow, hollow, or telephone-like. Both deep bass and airy highs are absent simultaneously.","<strong>Notch Filter (BRF):</strong> The sound retains its bass and its high sizzle, but sweeping the cutoff produces a \"whoosh\" or hollow phase dip through the midrange.","<strong>12 dB vs 24 dB Slope:</strong>","<strong>12 dB/oct (2-Pole):</strong> High harmonics are still softly audible even when the filter is closed down low (gentle, musical).","<strong>24 dB/oct (4-Pole):</strong> High harmonics are aggressively cut off (dark, abrupt, dramatic)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 3.12 (Filter Ear Training)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-3-3-8","title":"3.3.8 — Gate B: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.8 — Gate B: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before advancing to <strong>Module 4 (Envelopes: Shape Over Time)</strong>, verify your mastery of Module 3 filtering competencies."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                             GATE B CHECKLIST                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [ ] 1. Filter Type Ear Identification:                                        |\n|     Apply LPF, HPF, BPF, and Notch across an initialized Sawtooth wave.       |\n|     Can you identify all four types purely by ear with 100% accuracy?         |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 2. Slope Distinction:                                                     |\n|     Can you hear and explain the acoustic difference between a 12 dB/oct      |\n|     (2-pole) and 24 dB/oct (4-pole) low-pass filter slope?                    |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 3. Self-Oscillation Execution:                                           |\n|     Can you take an initialized Analog or Drift device, mute all oscillators, |\n|     crank filter resonance to 100%, and play a pitch-tracked sine wave melody?|\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 4. Mud Zone Diagnosis:                                                    |\n|     Can you identify the 200 Hz - 400 Hz frequency region on an EQ Eight and  |\n|     explain why excessive energy here damages mix clarity?                    |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 5. Keytracking Mastery:                                                   |\n|     Can you explain why 100% filter keytracking is required on polyphonic lead|\n|     and pad patches to prevent upper octave notes from sounding muffled?      |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"If all 5 criteria are verified by ear and manual execution, <strong>Gate B is passed</strong>. Proceed to <strong>Module 4</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound Design Foundations Syllabus</em>, §3 (Gate B Definition)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
